public class BlockedVsWaiting {
    static final Object lock = new Object();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // Holder takes the lock, then wait() — releases lock and goes WAITING
        Thread holder = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lock) {
                try { lock.wait(); }
                catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
            }
        });
        // Contender just tries to enter the block — gets BLOCKED if lock is held
        Thread contender = new Thread(() -> { synchronized (lock) { /* work */ } });

        holder.start();    Thread.sleep(100);
        contender.start(); Thread.sleep(100);
        System.out.println("holder:    " + holder.getState());    // WAITING
        System.out.println("contender: " + contender.getState()); // BLOCKED
        synchronized (lock) { lock.notify(); }
    }
}
```

BLOCKED means stuck at the lock door; WAITING means the thread released the lock voluntarily and needs a notify().

